# i-want-my-mtg scheduled jobs
SHELL=/bin/bash
HOME=/home/ubuntu
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# Every time below is UTC because the host runs Etc/UTC - not because cron is
# pinned to it. Do not "fix" that with a CRON_TZ=UTC line: Ubuntu's cron
# (3.0pl1-184ubuntu2) has no such setting - the string does not appear in
# /usr/sbin/cron at all - and `man 5 crontab` LIMITATIONS is explicit that a TZ
# set here "will affect only the commands executed in the crontab, not the
# execution of the crontab tasks themselves". Such a line would read as a
# guarantee while doing nothing.
#
# So the host timezone is load-bearing. If it ever moves off UTC these
# schedules move with it, and the ingest drifts off MTGJSON's ~06:10 UTC
# publish window - which is the exact failure the 08:00 slot below exists to
# avoid.

# Where cron mails job output.
#
# Every job below redirects stdout - and only stdout - to its own log. scry
# sends its whole report there via tracing and writes command failures to
# stderr, so the only thing that ever reaches this address is a failure: a
# non-zero exit, a run killed by its timeout, or a run skipped because the
# previous one is still holding the lock. Successful runs mail nothing.
#
# Do not add `2>&1` to these lines - that is what silenced two days of failed
# ingests. Requires a working local MTA.
MAILTO=legal@iwantmymtg.net

# MTGJSON pipeline (hourly, gated).
#
# Runs `scry has-new-prices` first - a 200-byte range read of the head of
# AllPricesToday.json - and does nothing unless upstream is serving data newer
# than ours. When it is, it runs ingest, then price alerts, then the portfolio
# summary, sequentially, because each depends on the one before it.
#
# This replaced four jobs on fixed times (ingest 08:00, price alerts 08:15,
# portfolio 08:30, retry 09:00). Every one of those times encoded a guess about
# when MTGJSON publishes, and the guess was wrong: a probe polling every 10
# minutes from 2026-08-28 saw no new build for 22 hours, nine of them past the
# assumed window. The 08:00 run kept downloading 550MB to rediscover the
# previous day's data and mailing about it, and the 09:00 retry never fired
# because `scry health` tolerates a one-day-old catalog.
#
# Hourly costs ~5KB a day and picks up a publish within the hour, whenever it
# lands. Nothing is mailed on the hours with nothing to do.
0 * * * * ubuntu /opt/scripts/ingest-chain.sh >> /var/log/i-want-my-mtg/ingestion.log

# Price history retention cleanup (weekly, Sunday at 3 AM)
0 3 * * 0 ubuntu /opt/scripts/scry.sh retention >> /var/log/i-want-my-mtg/retention.log

# Published tournament-deck ingest (daily at 3:30 AM; fetches the prior 2 days)
30 3 * * * ubuntu /opt/scripts/scry.sh ingest-decks --days 2 >> /var/log/i-want-my-mtg/decks.log

# Data freshness check (daily at 10:00 UTC, well after the ingest window).
# `scry health` exits non-zero when the newest price row is more than a day
# old. Prices carry MTGJSON's file date, not the ingest date; now that the
# ingest runs after the upstream publish, a healthy catalog checked here is
# same-day, and one day old means a single upstream build was skipped.
# The ingest is now hourly and gated, so there is no slot to sit after; this
# is simply a daily read of how fresh the catalog ended up.
0 10 * * * ubuntu /opt/scripts/scry.sh health >> /var/log/i-want-my-mtg/health.log

# Log cleanup (weekly, Sunday at 4 AM)
0 4 * * 0 ubuntu /opt/scripts/clean_logs.sh >> /var/log/i-want-my-mtg/cleanup.log

